+# BLOCKED: `002_unique_email.sql` cannot be applied to production data
+
+## What is blocked
+
+Making `users.email` required and case-insensitively unique **for the existing
+production data**. The constraint itself is implemented in
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` (rebuilt table with `email TEXT NOT NULL` and
+`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_lower_unique ON users (lower(email))`) and works
+on a fresh database — `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` passes.
+
+It cannot be applied to `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` (the anonymised 2026-10-01
+production snapshot) without changing or deleting customer rows, which is
+Support's data and explicitly out of scope for this migration.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The constraints have to hold for rows that already exist. The production
+snapshot contains two kinds of rows that violate them:
+
+1. **Case-insensitive duplicate emails** — no unique index on `lower(email)` can
+   be created while these coexist:
+
+   | `lower(email)` | ids |
+   | --- | --- |
+   | `ana.silva@example.com` | 1, 4, 23 |
+
+   ids 1 and 23 are the identical string `ana.silva@example.com`; id 4 is
+   `Ana.Silva@example.com`, which collides only under case-folding.
+
+2. **Missing email** — id 17 (`Goran Okafor`) has `email IS NULL`, so it cannot
+   satisfy `NOT NULL`.
+
+Resolving either requires deciding, per row, which duplicate to keep and what
+email the NULL row should have — i.e. updating or deleting customer rows. This
+migration is not allowed to do that, and the test correctly asserts that
+`id, email, name` are byte-for-byte identical before and after the migration.
+
+## Current state
+
+- `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` is written and correct for clean data. It
+  rebuilds the table inside a single transaction and copies `id, email, name`
+  unchanged.
+- On the production snapshot it fails with
+  `sqlite3.IntegrityError: NOT NULL constraint failed: users_new.email`, the
+  transaction rolls back, and all 40 rows (and the original schema) are left
+  untouched. Verified directly against the snapshot.
+- `test_migrations.py`: `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` passes;
+  `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` fails at `db.apply(conn, "002_unique_email.sql")`
+  because of the data conflicts above — not because of a bug in the migration.
+
+No workaround was added (triggers, partial indexes, or silently skipping
+offending rows). Those would leave existing duplicate/NULL rows in place while
+making the migration *look* applied, and would enforce a weaker rule than
+"required and unique".
+
+## What unblocks it
+
+Support needs to clean the data, then this migration can be applied unchanged:
+
+- Deduplicate ids 1, 4 and 23 (merge or delete, keeping the surviving account's
+  email) so no two rows share the same `lower(email)`.
+- Give id 17 an email address, or delete/merge that account.
+
+After that, re-run `test_migrations.py`; both tests should pass with no change
+to `002_unique_email.sql`.
